== Chapter II

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[Indwelling sin a law. In what sense it is so called. What kind of law it is. An inward effective principle called a law. The power of sin shown from this.]
]

#smallcaps[What] we have set out to consider is the #emph[power and efficacy of indwelling sin]. There are many ways in which it may be shown. I will begin with the #emph[name] given to it in the place mentioned before: it is a #emph[law]. “I find this law,” says the apostle. It is called so because of its power and efficacy. In the same way the principle of grace in believers is called the #emph[law of the Spirit of life], as we observed before (Rom 8:3), which is “the surpassing greatness of the power of God in them” (Eph 1:19). Where there is a #emph[law], there is power.

We will therefore show both what belongs to it as a #emph[law] in general, and also what is peculiar or proper to it as being #emph[such a law] as we have described.

In general, two things go with every #emph[law] as such.

First, #emph[dominion]. Rom 7:1: “The law has dominion over a man as long as he lives”; κυριεύει τοῦ ἀνθρώπου, it #emph[lords] it over a man. Wherever any law takes effect, κυριεύει, it has #emph[dominion]. It is properly the #emph[act of a superior], and it belongs to its nature to demand obedience by way of dominion. Now there is a twofold #emph[dominion], as there is a twofold law. There is a #emph[moral], authoritative dominion over a man, and there is a #emph[real, effective] dominion in a man. The first is a property of the law of God, the latter of the law of sin. The law of sin does not have in itself a #emph[moral dominion]\; it has no rightful dominion or authority over any man. But it has what is equivalent to it. That is why it is said βασιλεύειν, to #emph[reign] as a king (Rom 6:12), and κυριεύειν, to #emph[lord] it or have dominion (verse 14), as a law in general is said to have (Rom 7:1). But because it has lost its #emph[complete dominion] with respect to believers, of whom alone we speak, I will not dwell on it in this utmost extent of its power. But even #emph[in] them it is still a law, though not a #emph[law to] them. Yet, as was said, it is a law #emph[in] them. And though it does not have a complete and, as it were, a #emph[rightful dominion] over them, yet it will have a #emph[domination] as to some things in them. It is still a law, and a law in them, so that all its actions are the actions of a law. That is, it acts with power, though it has lost its complete power of ruling in them. Though it is #emph[weakened], its nature is not #emph[changed]. It is still a law, and therefore powerful. And as its particular workings (which we will consider later) are the ground of this name, so the term itself teaches us in general what we are to expect from it, and what efforts it will make for the #emph[dominion] it has been used to.

Secondly, a law, as a law, has an efficacy to #emph[move] those who are subject to it to the things it requires. A law has #emph[rewards] and #emph[punishments] that go with it. These secretly prevail on those to whom they are set out, even though the things commanded are not very desirable. And generally all laws have their efficacy on the minds of men from the #emph[rewards] and #emph[punishments] attached to them. Nor is this law without this spring of power: it has its rewards and punishments. The #emph[pleasures] of sin are the #emph[rewards] of sin, a reward that most men lose their souls to obtain. By this the law of sin contended against the law of grace in #emph[Moses] (Heb 11:25, 26): “He chose to suffer oppression with God’s people rather than to experience the fleeting pleasures of sin … for he was looking ahead to the recompense of the reward.” The contest in his mind was between the law of sin and the law of grace. On the side of the law of sin, the motive by which it sought to draw him over, and by which it prevails on most men, was the #emph[reward] it set before him, namely, that he would have the present enjoyment of the “pleasures of sin.” By this it contended against the reward attached to the law of grace, called the “recompense of the reward.”

By this #emph[sorry reward] this law keeps the world in obedience to its commands, and experience shows us how powerful it is to influence the minds of men. It also has #emph[punishments] with which it threatens those who labor to throw off its yoke. Whatever evil, trouble, or danger in the world goes with gospel obedience, and whatever hardship or violence must be done to the sensual part of our natures in a strict course of mortification, sin makes use of as if they were #emph[punishments] that follow the neglect of its commands. By these it prevails on the #emph[fearful], who will have no share in eternal life (Rev 21:8). And it is hard to say by which of these, its pretended rewards or its pretended punishments, it prevails most, and in which of them its greatest strength lies. By its #emph[rewards] it entices men to sins of commission, as they are called, in ways and actions that tend to satisfy its lusts. By its #emph[punishments] it leads men to omit duties, a course that tends to no less ruinous an outcome than the former. By which of these the law of sin has its greatest success in and on the souls of men is not plain, and that is because they are seldom or never separated, but take effect equally on the same persons. But this is certain. By offers and promises of the pleasures of sin on the one hand, and by threats of the loss of all sensual satisfactions and the infliction of temporal evils on the other, it has an exceeding efficacy on the minds of men, often on believers themselves. Unless a man is prepared to reject the reasonings that will offer themselves from the one and from the other of these, there is no standing before the #emph[power] of this law. The world falls before them every day. With what deceit and violence they are pressed and forced on the minds of men we will declare later, as well as what advantages they have to prevail on them. Look at the generality of men, and you will find them wholly at sin’s disposal by these means. Do the profits and pleasures of sin lie before them? Nothing can keep them from reaching after them. Do difficulties and inconveniences go with the duties of the gospel? They will have nothing to do with them. And so they are wholly given up to the rule and dominion of this law.

And this is the #emph[light] we have in general into the power and efficacy of indwelling sin from the general nature of a #emph[law], which it shares.

We may next consider what #emph[kind of law] in particular it is, which will further show the power of it that we are inquiring into. It is not an outward, written, commanding, directing #emph[law], but an inborn, working, driving, urging #emph[law]. A law #emph[set before] us is not to be compared for efficacy with a law #emph[inborn] in us. #emph[Adam] had a law of sin #emph[set before] him in his temptation, but because he had no law of sin #emph[inborn] and working in him, he might have withstood it. An #emph[inborn] law must be effectual. Let us take an example from the law that is contrary to this law of sin. The law of God was at first #emph[inborn] and natural to man. It was created together with his faculties, and was their #emph[rightness], both in being and in working, with respect to his end of living to God and glorifying him. So it had a special power in the whole soul to enable it to all obedience, and indeed to make all obedience #emph[easy] and pleasant. Such is the power of an inborn law. And though this law, as to its #emph[rule] and #emph[dominion], is now by nature cast out of the soul, yet its remaining sparks, because they are inborn, are very powerful and effectual, as the apostle declares (Rom 2:14, 15). Afterward God renews this law and writes it on tablets of stone. But what is the efficacy of this law? Now that it is external and set before men, will it enable them to perform the things it demands and requires? Not at all. God knew it would not, unless it were turned into an #emph[internal law] again, that is, until it was turned from a moral outward rule into an inward real principle. Therefore, when he intends to give his law power to produce obedience in his people, God makes it #emph[internal] again and plants it in the heart as it was at first (Jer 31:31, 32, 33): “I will put My law in their inward parts and inscribe it on their hearts.” This is what God fixes on, as it were on discovering that an outward law is not sufficient to lead men to obedience. The written law, he says, will not do it. Mercies and deliverances from distress will not bring it about. Trials and afflictions will not accomplish it. Then I will take another course, says the Lord. I will turn the #emph[written law] into an #emph[internal living principle] in their hearts, and that will have such efficacy that it will surely #emph[make] them my people, and #emph[keep] them so. Now such is this law of sin. It is an #emph[indwelling law] (Rom 7:17). It is “sin that dwells in me” (verse 20); “sin that dwells in me” (verse 21); it is “present with me” (verse 23); it is “in my members.” Indeed, it is so far within a man that in some sense it is said to be the man himself (verse 18): “I know that nothing good dwells in me, that is, in my flesh.” The #emph[flesh], which is the seat and throne of this law, and which indeed is this law, is in some sense the man himself, as #emph[grace] also is the new man. Now from this consideration of it, that it is an #emph[indwelling law] inclining and moving to sin, as an inward habit or principle, it has various advantages that increase its strength and further its power. As,

First, it always #emph[abides] in the soul; it is never absent. The apostle twice uses the expression, it #emph[dwells] in me. There is its constant residence and home. If it #emph[came] on the soul only at certain times, much obedience might be perfectly accomplished in its absence. Indeed, as men deal with #emph[usurping tyrants] whom they intend to drive out of a city, the gates might sometimes be shut against it so that it could not return. The soul might #emph[fortify] itself against it. But the soul is its #emph[home]\; there it #emph[dwells], and it is no wanderer. Wherever you are, whatever you are doing, this law of sin is always #emph[in you]: in the best that you do, and in the worst. Men little consider what a dangerous companion is always at #emph[home] with them. When they are in company or alone, by night or by day, it is all one: sin is with them. There is a live #emph[coal] continually in their houses, which, if it is not watched, will #emph[set fire] to them and perhaps #emph[consume] them. Oh, the woeful carnal security of poor souls! How little do most men think of this #emph[inborn] enemy that is never away from home! How little, for the most part, does the #emph[watchfulness] of any professors answer to the danger of their state and condition!

Secondly, it is always #emph[ready] to apply itself to every end and purpose that it serves. It does not only “dwell in me,” says the apostle, “but when I want to do good, it is present with me.” There is something more in that expression than mere #emph[indwelling]. A #emph[lodger] may live in a house and yet not always be meddling with what the master of the house has to do (to keep to the picture of #emph[indwelling] used by the apostle). But with this law it is otherwise. It so #emph[dwells] in us that it will be #emph[present with us] in everything we do. Indeed, often when we most #emph[earnestly] desire to be rid of it, it will force itself on us with most #emph[violence]. “When I want to do good, it is present with me.” Would you pray? Would you hear the word? Would you give alms? Would you meditate? Would you be in any duty, exercising faith in God and love toward him? Would you work righteousness? Would you resist temptations? This troublesome, perplexing #emph[indweller] will still force itself on you more or less, and be present with you, so that you cannot perfectly and completely accomplish the thing that is good, as our apostle speaks in verse 18. Sometimes men stir up, excite and provoke their lusts by listening to their temptations; and no wonder if then they find them #emph[present] and active. But it will be so even when we labor with all our efforts to be free from them. This law of sin dwells in us; that is, it clings as a depraved principle to our #emph[minds] in #emph[darkness] and vanity, to our affections in sensuality, and to our wills in a loathing of and aversion from what is good. And by some, more, or all of these it is continually forcing itself on us, in inclinations, motions, or suggestions to evil, when we would most gladly be rid of it.

Thirdly, since it is an indwelling law, it applies itself to its work with great #emph[ease] and readiness, like the sin that so “easily besets” us (Heb 12:1). It has great #emph[ease] and readiness in applying itself to its work. It needs no doors to be opened to it; it needs no machines to work with. The soul cannot apply itself to any duty of a man except by the exercise of those #emph[faculties] in which this law has its residence. Is the understanding or the #emph[mind] to be applied to anything? There it is, in ignorance, darkness, vanity, folly, madness. Is the #emph[will] to be engaged? There it is also, in spiritual deadness, stubbornness, and the roots of obstinacy. Are the heart and #emph[affections] to be set to work? There it is, in inclinations to the world and present things, and sensuality, with a proneness to every kind of defilement. So it is #emph[easy] for it to work its way into all that we do, to hinder all that is good, and to further all sin and wickedness. It has an #emph[intimacy], an inwardness with the soul, and therefore it easily besets us in all that we do. It possesses the very faculties of the soul by which we must do whatever we do, good or evil. Now it has all these advantages as it is a #emph[law], an #emph[indwelling law], and they show its power and efficacy. It is always resident in the soul; it forces itself on all the soul’s actions, and that with ease and readiness.

This is the #emph[law] that the apostle says he #emph[found] in himself. This is the title he gives to the powerful and effectual remainders of indwelling sin even in believers. And these are the general evidences of its power that we have from that name. There are many in the world who do not #emph[find] this law in themselves. Whatever they have been taught in the word, they have no spiritual sense and experience of the power of indwelling sin, and that is because they are wholly under its dominion. They do not find that there is #emph[darkness] and folly in their minds, because they are darkness itself, and darkness will discover nothing. They do not find #emph[deadness] and unfitness toward God in their hearts and wills, because they are wholly #emph[dead] in trespasses and sins. They are at peace with their lusts by being in bondage to them. And this is the state of most men in the world, which makes them woefully despise everything that concerns them eternally. Why is it that men follow and pursue the world with so much greed that they neglect heaven, and life, and immortality for it every day? Why is it that some pursue their sensuality with delight, and will drink, and revel, and have their amusements, let others say what they please? Why is it that so many live so unprofitably under the word, understand so little of what is spoken to them, practice still less of what they understand, and will by no means be stirred up to answer the mind of God in his calls to them? All these things come from this law of sin and its power, which rules and holds sway in men. But it is not such persons that we are particularly treating at present.

From what has been said it follows that if there is such a law in believers, it is without doubt their duty to #emph[find] it out, to find that it is so.

The more they find its power, the less they will feel its effects. It will do a man no good at all to have a #emph[wasting] fever and not discover it, or a fire lying hidden in his house and not know it. As much as men find of this law in themselves, so much they will #emph[abhor] it and themselves, and no more. Their #emph[earnestness] for grace will also be in proportion to their discovery of it, and will rise no higher. All watchfulness and #emph[diligence] in obedience will also correspond to it. The whole course of our lives turns on this one hinge: finding out and experiencing the power and efficacy of this law of sin. Ignorance of it breeds senselessness, carelessness, sloth, carnal security and pride, all of which the Lord’s soul abhors. Outbreaks into great, open, conscience-wasting, scandalous sins come from the lack of a due spiritual consideration of this law. Inquire, then, how it is with your#super[+] souls. What do you#super[+] find of this law? What experience do you#super[+] have of its power and efficacy? Do you#super[+] find it dwelling in you#super[+], always present with you#super[+], stirring itself up, or putting out its poison with ease and readiness, at all times, in all your#super[+] duties, “when you#super[+] want to do good”? What humiliation, what self-abasement, what intensity in prayer, what diligence, what watchfulness does this call for from you#super[+]? What spiritual wisdom do you#super[+] need? What supplies of grace, what help of the Holy Spirit, will this also show you#super[+] need? I fear that few of us have a diligence in proportion to our danger.
